.TH MAKE_GORM 1 "July 2026" "Gershwin Desktop Environment" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
make_gorm \- Gorm text archive compiler/decompiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B make_gorm
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I INPUT
.I OUTPUT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B make_gorm
converts between binary Gorm archive files (.gorm) and a deterministic
textual representation (.gormt) suitable for version control.
.PP
Gorm is the GNUstep interface builder. Its document format (.gorm) is a
directory bundle containing a binary GNUstep archive (objects.gorm) along
with metadata files. Editing .gorm files directly is impractical because
the binary format makes diffs opaque and merges impossible. Editing the
textual .gormt representation solves this.
.PP
.B make_gorm
performs a
.I lossless
conversion: a binary .gorm file survives repeated binary\(rarrtext\(rarrbinary
conversions without semantic changes. The canonical textual output is
deterministic and stable, producing minimal diffs when properties change.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B decompile
Convert a binary .gorm to text .gormt:
.RS
.PP
make_gorm decompile MainMenu.gorm MainMenu.gormt
.RE
.PP
If the input is a directory (standard .gorm bundle), the tool reads
objects.gorm from inside it.
.TP
.B compile
Convert text .gormt back to binary .gorm:
.RS
.PP
make_gorm compile MainMenu.gormt MainMenu.gorm
.RE
.PP
The output is a .gorm bundle directory containing objects.gorm,
data.classes, and data.info.
.TP
.B verify
Validate a binary .gorm file:
.RS
.PP
make_gorm verify MainMenu.gorm
.RE
.PP
Reports object count, class count, and archive version. Exits with
non-zero status if the file is invalid.
.TP
.B canonicalize
Normalize a .gormt file in place (sorts objects by ID, properties
alphabetically, etc.):
.RS
.PP
make_gorm canonicalize MainMenu.gormt
.RE
.SH TEXT FORMAT
The .gormt format is UTF-8 with Unix line endings and four-space
indentation. Each object is serialized with its class name and a raw
hexadecimal data block preserving the encoded properties:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gorm-text 1

metadata
{
    archiveVersion = 1000000;
    coderVersion = 2;
}

object 1
{
    class = GSNibContainer;
    data = <data>
        48656C6C6F20576F726C64
    </data>;
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
The hex data preserves every property, object reference, cyclic
reference, and shared reference from the original binary archive.
.SH PROPERTIES
.TP
Lossless round-trip
Binary .gorm \(rarr .gormt \(rarr .gorm produces byte-identical text
output after the first cycle.
.TP
Canonical output
Objects sorted by ID, properties sorted alphabetically, hex uppercase,
64 bytes per line, no trailing whitespace.
.TP
Git-friendly
Changing one property (e.g., a button title) produces a one-line diff:
.RS
.PP
.nf
-       "Cancel"
+       "Close"
.RE
.fi
.TP
Bundle support
Reads and writes .gorm bundle directories (objects.gorm inside).
.SH GORMTEXT FORMAT SPECIFICATION
.PP
The .gormt format captures the full structure of a GNUstep binary archive:
.TP
String values
Enclosed in double quotes, with standard escape sequences (\\n, \\t,
\\\\).
.TP
Numeric values
Integers and floating-point numbers written without unnecessary zeros.
.TP
Boolean values
true or false.
.TP
Null
null.
.TP
Object references
@I{D} (e.g., @4 references object with ID 4).
.TP
Binary data
<data> ... </data> with uppercase hex, 64 bytes per line.
.TP
Arrays
[ value, value, ... ]
.TP
Dictionaries
{ key = value; key2 = value2; } with keys sorted alphabetically.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Decompile a .gorm bundle into text for version control:
.PP
.nf
.RS
make_gorm decompile MyWindow.gorm MyWindow.gormt
git add MyWindow.gormt
git commit -m "update window layout"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Compile text back to .gorm for building:
.PP
.nf
.RS
make_gorm compile MyWindow.gormt MyWindow.gorm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use in a GNUmakefile for build-time compilation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
MyWindow.gorm: MyWindow.gormt
${CODE}(make_gorm) $${CODE}< $@
.RE
.fi
.PP
Verify an archive before deployment:
.PP
.nf
.RS
make_gorm verify Resources/MainMenu.gorm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Canonicalize a hand-edited .gormt file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
make_gorm canonicalize MyWindow.gormt
.RE
.fi
.PP
Initial setup: extract all .gorm files in a project to text:
.PP
.nf
.RS
find Resources -name "*.gorm" -type d | while read f; do
  make_gorm decompile "$f" "${f}.txt"
done
git add Resources/*.gorm.txt
git commit -m "initial gorm text extraction"
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (invalid input, syntax error, missing file).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I INPUT\fR / \fIOUTPUT
Paths to .gorm or .gormt files. Standard .gorm bundles are directories
containing at minimum an objects.gorm file.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP
Named properties
The tool outputs raw hex data by default. To output named properties
(requires a running display server), compile with -DRECORDING_CODER.
This mode is experimental and may crash on complex view hierarchies.
.TP
Unknown classes
Classes not available at runtime are preserved in the hex data but
cannot be introspected for named property output.
